---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import AboutVolumeAtlas from "../../components/AboutVolumeAtlas.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../../lib/paths";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Mission and editorial principles of Independent Observer."
  currentPath="/about/"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="About the project"
    title="Independent Observer"
    description="A public research project about how evidence, institutions, history, work, science, and technology shape what people can know, contest, and change."
    className="about-hero"
  />
  <section class="section section-navy" aria-labelledby="about-program-title">
    <div class="shell page-grid">
      <aside class="page-aside">
        <span>Four volumes</span>
        <span>One research program</span>
        <span>Independent Observer</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="about-program-title">One project. Four volumes.</h2>
        <p>
          Independent Observer studies how public life becomes legible: what the record shows, which
          institutions shape it, who carries the cost, and what kinds of capability can be governed.
          The project is grounded in identifiable sources and visible uncertainty; it is not a
          conspiracy catalogue or a single conclusion.
        </p>
        <div class="mission-volume-list" aria-label="The four Independent Observer volumes">
          <article class="mission-volume">
            <span class="mission-volume-number">Volume I</span>
            <h3><a href={sitePath("/series/independent-observer/")}>Independent Observer</a></h3>
            <p>
              Observation and evidence: how a public claim is documented, tested, and kept
              correctable.
            </p>
            <a class="mission-volume-link" href={sitePath("/series/independent-observer/")}
              >Open Volume I →</a
            >
          </article>
          <article class="mission-volume">
            <span class="mission-volume-number">Volume II</span>
            <h3>
              <a href={sitePath("/series/the-empire-beneath-democracy/")}
                >The Empire Beneath Democracy</a
              >
            </h3>
            <p>
              Institutions and sovereignty: how formal democracy meets practical power, membership,
              and enforcement.
            </p>
            <a class="mission-volume-link" href={sitePath("/series/the-empire-beneath-democracy/")}
              >Open Volume II →</a
            >
          </article>
          <article class="mission-volume">
            <span class="mission-volume-number">Volume III</span>
            <h3><a href={sitePath("/series/managed-decline/")}>Managed Decline</a></h3>
            <p>
              Work and social citizenship: who absorbs economic change and who can access public
              capacity.
            </p>
            <a class="mission-volume-link" href={sitePath("/series/managed-decline/")}
              >Open Volume III →</a
            >
          </article>
          <article class="mission-volume">
            <span class="mission-volume-number">Volume IV</span>
            <h3>
              <a href={sitePath("/series/the-last-human-workforce/")}>The Last Human Workforce</a>
            </h3>
            <p>
              Science and capability: how AI, infrastructure, and human adaptation become governable
              capacity.
            </p>
            <a class="mission-volume-link" href={sitePath("/series/the-last-human-workforce/")}
              >Open Volume IV →</a
            >
          </article>
        </div>
        <p>
          The sequence is deliberate: observation makes power legible, power explains distribution,
          distribution shapes capability, and capability returns the project to the question of who
          can observe, participate, and correct the system.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="#about-volume-atlas">Browse the interactive volume atlas →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-white">
    <div class="shell page-grid">
      <aside class="page-aside">Mission</aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Look past the approved frame.</h2>
        <p>
          Public controversies are often presented as isolated disputes between personalities or
          parties. This project instead asks how rules, incentives, historical memory, technology,
          and institutional structure shape the available choices.
        </p>
        <p>
          Independent Observer is a connected four-volume inquiry. Each volume carries the same
          method into a different part of public life, so the project can examine not only what
          happened, but the conditions that made it visible, durable, or difficult to change.
        </p>
        <p>
          Read the four volumes as a sequence: Volume I establishes how to observe; Volume II asks
          where authority and sovereignty are organized; Volume III examines work, taxation, and
          social citizenship; Volume IV tests technological change against human capability and asks
          who gets to govern it.
        </p>
        <p>
          “Independent” does not mean free from values, error, or perspective. It means the work
          should not conceal its reasoning behind tribal loyalty. Claims should be sourced,
          interpretations should be identifiable, and uncertainty should remain visible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <AboutVolumeAtlas />
</BaseLayout>
